[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `main` | `else` → `bad()` | `zero_takes_the_bad_branch` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `main` | `scanf` succeeds, assigns `x` | all good/bad-branch tests |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `main` | `scanf` input failure (EOF before any non-whitespace), `x` untouched | `empty_input_leaves_x_at_zero`, `whitespace_only_input_is_an_input_failure` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `main` | `scanf` matching failure (no digits), `x` untouched | `non_numeric_input_is_a_matching_failure`, `sign_without_digits_is_a_matching_failure` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bad()`, `good()`, `helperBad()` and `helperGood1()` are straight-line and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unconditional; they are covered by the two `main` branches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There is no `argv` handling, no `fgets`, no `stderr` write, and no `return`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m other than `main`'s `return 0`, so exit status is 0 on every path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Verified behaviors that a naive translation would have gotten wrong
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `helperBad()` returns NULL, not a dangling pointer — so `bad()` prints nothing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `helperBad` declares `char charString[]` with automatic storage and returns its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m address. This is the CWE-562 defect the test case exists to demonstrate, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m its result is not "print garbage": GCC diagnoses it and compiles the return
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value as a literal zero. Confirmed from the reference binary:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ objdump -d --no-show-raw-insn c_src/build/driver | sed -n '/<helperBad>:/,/ret/p'
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0000000000401158 <helperBad>:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   mov    %rax,-0x20(%rbp)      # buffer is still written to the stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   mov    %rdx,-0x18(%rbp)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   movb   $0x0,-0x10(%rbp)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   mov    $0x0,%eax             # ...and then discarded; returns NULL
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   pop    %rbp
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ret
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Build warning, for the record:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/src/main.c:37:12: warning: function returns address of local variable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    [-Wreturn-local-addr]
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printLine`'s NULL guard therefore suppresses output entirely. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `helper_bad()` returns `None` to reproduce this. Two plausible translations are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m wrong here and both would have been caught by `zero_takes_the_bad_branch`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - returning the string (`Some("helperBad string")`) — the C prints **nothing**,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so this adds a spurious 19-byte line on stdout;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - printing an empty line — the NULL branch of `printLine` skips the `\n` too,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so stdout must be completely empty.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `scanf` failure leaves `x` at its initializer `0`, which routes to `bad()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%d", &x)` does not write to `x` on either input or matching failure, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C ignores the return value. `x` keeps the `int x = 0;` initializer and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `else` branch runs. The Rust mirrors this by only overwriting `x` when the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m conversion succeeds:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m let mut x: i32 = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if let Some(value) = scanner.scan_int() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     x = value;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A translation that treated a parse failure as an error (nonzero exit, a message
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m on stderr, or a panic) would diverge on empty input, `"abc"`, `"-"`, `"+"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `".5"` and every whitespace-only input. Note that a stdout-only comparison would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *not* catch the exit-status half of that divergence, which is why every case
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m asserts all three streams.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. `scanf` skips whitespace across newlines; it is not line-oriented
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%d` skips any run of whitespace, newlines included, before the first digit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"\n\n\n\n7"` yields 7 and prints, and `"  \n\t\n 42  extra"` yields 42. A
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fgets`- or `read_line`-based translation would see an empty first line and fail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the conversion, wrongly taking the bad branch. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf_reads_across_newlines`, which includes all six C-locale whitespace bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (space, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. Overflow saturates at the `long` range, then truncates to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc converts `%d` with `strtol` semantics — clamping to `LONG_MAX` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `LONG_MIN` on overflow — and the store into `int x` truncates to the low 32
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bits. This makes the branch taken counter-intuitive for several inputs, all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed against the C binary:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin | conversion result | `x` after truncation | branch |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` (2^32) | 4294967296 | 0 | **bad** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `12884901888` (3·2^32) | 12884901888 | 0 | **bad** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-4294967296` | −4294967296 | 0 | **bad** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372032559808512` (0x7fffffff00000000) | itself | 0 | **bad** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775808` (LONG_MIN) | LONG_MIN | 0 | **bad** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-999…9` (40 nines) | LONG_MIN (saturated) | 0 | **bad** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` (2^31) | 2147483648 | −2147483648 | good |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967295` | 4294967295 | −1 | good |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `18446744073709551616` (2^64) | LONG_MAX (saturated) | −1 | good |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `999…9` (40 nines) | LONG_MAX (saturated) | −1 | good |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two distinguishing observations pin down the semantics rather than assuming
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m them. Input `2^64` prints, which rules out modular wraparound (wrapping would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m give `x == 0` and no output) and confirms saturation at `LONG_MAX`. Input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `4294967296` prints nothing, which confirms the narrowing store to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m actually truncates rather than saturating at `INT_MAX`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `values_truncating_to_zero_take_the_bad_branch`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `values_truncating_to_nonzero_take_the_good_branch` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `powers_of_two_and_neighbours`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. Conversion stops at the first character that cannot extend the number
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"0x10"` converts as `0` and stops at `x` (bad branch, not 16); `"3.14"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m converts as `3`; `"1abc"` as `1`; `"5-3"` as `5`. Trailing input is never read.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A translation that read a whole token or a whole line and then required it to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m parse in full would reject these instead of converting the prefix. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `conversion_stops_at_first_non_digit`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. A sign must be followed immediately by a digit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"- 5"` and `"+ 9"` are matching failures, not −5 and 9: whitespace is skipped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m only *before* the sign, never between the sign and the digits. Likewise `"--9"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `"+-3"` fail. Covered by `sign_without_digits_is_a_matching_failure`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. Digit runs far longer than any integer type
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5,000-digit inputs (all zeros, all nines, negative nines, zeros followed by a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m significant digit) match. `"0"×5000` is a valid conversion to 0 and takes the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bad branch; `"0"×5000 + "1"` converts to 1 and prints. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `very_long_digit_runs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. NUL and non-ASCII bytes are ordinary non-digits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `\0`, `\xff` and UTF-8 lead bytes are neither whitespace nor digits, so they end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whitespace skipping and cause a matching failure if they appear before any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m digit. `"5\0"` converts to 5 normally. The Rust scanner works on raw bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rather than `&str`, so no UTF-8 validation can reject input the C accepts —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `all_bytes` feeds it every byte 0x00–0xff in one input and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `every_single_byte_input` feeds each byte alone. Covered by `non_text_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 9. Write failures are ignored, exit status stays 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C ignores `printf`'s return value, so a closed or broken stdout still exits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0. The Rust discards the `write!` and `flush()` results (`let _ = ...`) for the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same reason; using `.unwrap()` or `println!` would panic or abort instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Checked out of band, since Cargo's test harness always supplies a live stdout:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 1 | c_src/build/driver 1>&-                       ; echo rc=$?   # rc=0, empty stderr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 1 | translation/target/release/driver 1>&-        ; echo rc=$?   # rc=0, empty stderr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 1 | c_src/build/driver | true                     ; echo rc=$?   # rc=0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 1 | translation/target/release/driver | true      ; echo rc=$?   # rc=0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Environment dependency worth flagging
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Item 1 is the one behavior that is a property of the *reference build* rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than of the C language: `helperBad()` returning the address of a stack local is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m undefined behavior, and GCC's choice to substitute NULL is what makes the bad
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m branch silent. A different compiler or optimization level could return the live
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stack address instead, in which case the C would print `helperBad string` (or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m garbage) and the Rust `None` would no longer match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The test suite is self-correcting on this point rather than hard-coding an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m expectation: it compares against whatever `c_src/build/driver` does, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m toolchain whose codegen differs would surface as a failure in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `zero_takes_the_bad_branch` rather than as a silently wrong pass. Verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against the GCC and default CMake configuration present in this working
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m directory.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Reproduction
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd ../../translation && cargo build --release && cargo test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
