The behavior that matters most: [38;5;10mhelperBad()[0m returns the address of a stack local, and GCC compiles that return as a literal zero ([38;5;10mmov $0x0,%eax[0m, confirmed by disassembly, alongside the [38;5;10m-Wreturn-local-addr[0m warning). So [38;5;10mbad()[0m prints [3mnothing at all[23m — not even a newline — and the existing [38;5;10mhelper_bad() -> None[0m is right. Also confirmed empirically rather than assumed: [38;5;10m%d[0m saturates at the [38;5;10mlong[0m range and then truncates to [38;5;10mint[0m, so [38;5;10m4294967296[0m takes the bad branch while [38;5;10m18446744073709551616[0m takes the good one.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mPhase D[22m — [38;5;10mcargo test[0m passes in both debug and release, nothing is [38;5;10m#[ignore][0md, and [38;5;10mc_src/[0m sources are byte-identical (only [38;5;10mc_src/build/[0m was added). I wiped both build trees and re-ran from scratch to confirm the suite bootstraps the C reference itself. As a negative control I temporarily made [38;5;10mhelper_bad()[0m return the string: 13 of 17 tests failed, then I restored the file and re-verified green — so the suite is detecting behavior, not passing vacuously.[0m[0m
